{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261030T014656Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "3f0d977e95f77e1fd5388ba6", "title": "Unlocking Tensor Core Performance for Machine Learning Interatomic Potentials", "url": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/unlocking-tensor-core-performance-for-machine-learning-interatomic-potentials/", "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog", "published": "2026-10-29T15:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.94, "worthiness": 0.92, "worthiness_confidence": 0.73, "final_score": 0.92, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 13626, "delivered": true, "summary": "NVIDIA demonstrated that Tensor Core FP32 methods (BF16x9 on Blackwell, TF32x3 on Ampere) accelerate machine learning interatomic potentials without retraining or architectural changes. Testing on Point Edge Transformer achieved 1.5–1.7x MD-step speedups while maintaining FP32-level accuracy for forces, energy conservation, and structural relaxation. This matters because MLIPs require numerical precision for stable long-timescale simulations, and these methods unlock higher Tensor Core throughput while preserving physical consistency. Key takeaway: enable these via environment variables or CUTLASS kernels for immediate performance gains on GEMM-dominated scientific workloads.", "jev_ms": 119.9, "jev_input_tokens": 3821, "jev_cost": 0.000160482, "llm_ms": 2203.4, "llm_cost": 0.004477, "errors": []}
